### Block 11 — FAQ

**H2:** Frequently asked questions

**Q: What is the difference between Model A and Model B in the proxy?**
Model A is the Executor Lobe. It calls functions, interacts with users or scripts, and holds code-execution privileges. Model B is the Strategist Lobe. It has no tool-mutation privileges, acts as a background auditor, and — in Director Mode (`lobe-a-director`) — takes conversational control to ask follow-up questions and guide Model A.

**Q: How does multi-agent recipient routing prevent agents from talking over each other?**
When you pass `X-DL-Floor-ID`, the proxy analyzes incoming multi-agent turns. If a speaker is addressing another agent or entity, Lobe B suppresses Model A from generating a spurious reply — while still committing the conversation into background memory, so context is never lost.

**Q: What happens if the review worker fails or runs slowly?**
The state endpoint reflects it explicitly: `oversight_status: degraded` or `stale`. Normal Mode responses are never held hostage to background evaluation lag. Note the trade-off honestly: review coverage can degrade silently from the user's point of view even though latency is unaffected — one measured session degraded on three of six reviews.

**Q: Does this require proprietary model weights?**
No. Dual-Lobe runs as an OpenAI-compatible reverse proxy in front of standard foundation models. It provides the dual-process architecture and verification runtime around any supported provider.

**Q: Is this a prompt or an architecture?**
An architecture. The four locks are enforced at the dispatch and state-machine layer, tool ownership is exhaustive and exclusive, inter-lobe messages are schema-validated, and the ledger is append-only. A wrong-owner tool call returns `ToolAccessDenied` and is logged.

**Q: Does Dual-Lobe prove the model is telling the truth?**
No, and it never claims to. The Strategist's job is to check material claims against the supplied record and label what is unsupported, contradictory, or unexplained. Missing evidence is not proof of fabrication. Confidence or tone alone is never a trigger for an accusation.

**Q: What does it cost?**
Reviews add a bounded second inference per eligible call (`DUAL_LOBE_B_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS` default 1400), plus optional state lookups and extra prompt tokens for memory and findings. Normal Mode never waits for the Strategist, but shared upstream capacity can still slow the Executor. Director Mode intentionally adds serial A/B calls, time, and cost.

**Q: What is it not?**
It is not a solution for malicious or systematically biased underlying models, collusion between two models with correlated training failures, compromised tools or forged evidence, policy errors encoded in the verifier, social or legal authorization questions, prompt injection without separate defenses, or the security of credentials and runtime infrastructure.

