## 11. [PROMO] Launch announcement (long-form blog / LinkedIn / Reddit r/LocalLLaMA post)

**Title:** I built an AI agent architecture where the model has to show its work

**Body:**
Every agent developer knows the feeling. The agent says the tests passed. The tests did not pass. You spend an hour and a half finding out, then another hour understanding why the summary was so confident.

The usual fix is to ask the model to check itself. That does not work, and the reason is structural: when an agent self-checks, it reads its own tokens through the same attention weights that produced them. It reviews a plausible story with a plausible story. Agreement, not verification.

Dual-Lobe takes a different route. It splits one task across two model processes with deliberately unequal powers.

The **Executor Lobe** acts. It streams responses and runs tools with zero critical-path wait. Your app keeps executing its tools exactly as before.

The **Strategist Lobe** senses, critiques, authorizes, and verifies. It has no tool-mutation privileges. In Normal Mode it reviews through a durable outbox, after delivery, off the critical path. In Director Mode it takes the conversation, asks follow-up questions, and directs another Executor turn — a visible A/B exchange.

Around both sit four hard invariants: a **Planning Lock** (no plan is active until the Strategist signs it), an **Action Lock** (side effects are blocked until permitted), a **Verification Lock** (an action is not complete until evidence is verified), and an **Escalation Lock** (recover internally before bothering the user).

And one rule runs through all of it: **narrow evidence may support only a narrow claim.**

When the Executor says the suite passed and the record shows `exit 1` and a checksum mismatch, the Strategist labels it `CONTRADICTION` and quotes both sides. When the Executor claims a migration "completed with validation" and the record shows three of five tables and no schema diff, it labels the validation portion `UNSUPPORTED`. When certainty jumps with nothing in the record to explain it, it labels a `SUSPICIOUS_SHIFT`. These are model assessments, always advisory — missing evidence is not proof of lying.

It is a drop-in reverse proxy. `docker compose up -d`, point your SDK at `localhost:8801`, pick `lobe-a`. Or `lobe-a-director` for the visible loop. Shared memory carries verified context across apps with `X-DL-Memory-ID`. Multi-agent rooms stop talking over each other with `X-DL-Floor-ID`. Every review is inspectable at `/v1/dual-lobe/state/{run_id}`.

Two things I want to be blunt about, because the whole project is about not overclaiming.

First, "reviewed" does not mean "true." It means the Strategist's output contract passed validation.

Second, in a published six-turn measured session, latency was unaffected (median time-to-first-token 587 ms with the proxy vs 626 ms direct) and replies were terser with accuracy preserved — but the Strategist's own JSON review failed validation on three of six turns and one state read failed. Fail-open hid it from the user. That is the weakest link, and it is documented rather than buried, because an anti-deception system that hides its own failure rate would be a joke.

If you are building long-running agents and you are tired of phantom green checkmarks, the repos are open. The research specification publishes its baselines and falsification criteria; the proxy is the working observation layer. Try it, break it, and tell me where the Strategist is wrong.

**Suggested closing line:**
> Evidence before success — and if there is no receipt, it is not a result.

